  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 2.2. Increased capacity of the member States to apply international labour standards</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">CEACR Observation on C 87:  
In its previous comments, the Committee had requested the Government to take the necessary measures to amend sections 20, 21 and 38 of the Law on Trade Unions (LTU) &#x2013; requiring those wishing to vote, to stand as a candidate for election, or be designated to leadership or management positions in unions or employer associations to meet a minimum age requirement (18), minimum literacy requirements and make a declaration that they have never been convicted for any criminal offence. On the one hand, the Committee notes with satisfaction that the 2019 amendments to the LTU removed the requirements of making a declaration that they have never been convicted of any criminal offense and, as to Khmer nationals, the literacy requirement.

 Since 2011, the Office has been providing technical assistance and expertise to the Government on the amendment of the Law on Trade Unions (LTU).  On 25 May 2011, the Office provided an ILO Memorandum of technical comments on the draft law on Trade Unions to the Government of Cambodia.  The Memorandum proposed a number of amendments on different articles of the draft Law including Article 21 (1)C.  In this connection, the Office drew the Government&apos;s attention to the fact that a law which prohibits access to trade union office because of any conviction is incompatible with the principles of freedom of association, when the activity condemned is not prejudicial to the aptitude and integrity required to exercise trade union office. Consequently, the Office suggested a redrafted provision to reflect the abovementioned principle.  A follow up mission by experts from the Office was carried out in 2018 at a tripartite workshop so as to present, explain and discuss the content of the Technical Memorandum with the Minister of Labour and Vocational Training of Cambodia and the social partners. This resulted in the amendment of the Law on Trade Unions in line with the recommendation of the Technical Memorandum and with the supervisory bodies&apos; recommendations.</narrative>
   </description>
